cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "PolicyJEV: zero-shot TypeSafe JEV versus trained baselines on toxic-comment classification and moderation decisions, under a frozen evaluation protocol"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Rana
    given-names: Muhammad Abbas
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-7585-7509"
version: 1.0.1
date-released: 2026-10-16
license: MIT
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23407397
repository-code: "https://github.com/AbbasRana001/PolicyJEV"
abstract: >-
  A comparison of zero-shot TypeSafe JEV (jev-1.13.0) with a TF-IDF logistic
  regression and a fine-tuned DistilBERT on the Jigsaw Toxic Comment Classification
  Challenge test set (63,978 comments), run under a frozen evaluation protocol. The
  primary comparison was fixed before test scoring. The moderation policy layer (a
  three-way policy: allow, human review, action, with thresholds fitted on validation
  data) was written after the test predictions existed and the test average precision, F1,
  calibration and review-budget results had been seen, and was frozen before the policy
  was evaluated on the test set. On test, JEV has the lowest mean average precision of
  the three systems, and every system missed some of the validation targets on test. The repository contains the protocol, frozen-file manifest, metrics,
  predictions, policy engine and a React and FastAPI console. It contains no comment text.
keywords:
  - toxicity classification
  - content moderation
  - zero-shot classification
  - frozen evaluation protocol
  - calibration
  - reproducible research
